    <DataNode elementId="AT1G44446-MONOMER" textLabel="chlorophyllide a oxygenase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9MBA1" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; enzyme, encoded by the &lt;i&gt;AtCAO&lt;/i&gt; gene, was shown through the study of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; mutants to be involved in the biosynthesis of chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[10468639]|. Although the protein was initially thought to be a chlorophyll a oxygenase, it was recently shown to catalyze the hydrozylation of chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; rather than that of chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; and has therefore been termed chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; oxygenase (CAO) |CITS:[16113212]|. This enzyme catalyzes an unusual two-step monooxygenase conversion of chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; to chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt; &lt;i&gt;via&lt;/i&gt; 7-OH-chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[16113212]|. Small amounts of this compound were detected when the recombinant CAO was expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;. No trace of &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;-compounds were found when chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; and protochlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; were used as substrates with the recombinant enzyme. Study of the &lt;i&gt;AtCAO&lt;/i&gt; mutants shown that Rieske-, as well as the mononuclear non-heme Fe-binding sites are important for proper gene function |CITS:[10468639]|. Partial deletion of &lt;i&gt;AtCAO&lt;/i&gt; results in the complete absence of chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G44446-MONOMER" />
